    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "shop vac vs dust extractor",
      "dust extractor",
      "HEPA dust extraction",
      "cyclone separator",
      "silica dust"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Shop Vac Picks",
    link: "/best-shop-vac/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Comparison Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Field Tested
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Shop Vac vs <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Dust Extractor</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          One collects debris. The other controls dust while you are making it. The gap is not power — it is whether the machine still performs an hour into a sanding job.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#the-difference"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See the Core Difference
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#the-difference" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Core Difference</a>
          <a href="#filter-cleaning" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Auto Filter Cleaning</a>
          <a href="#filtration-standards" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Filtration Standards</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. What Each Costs</a>
          <a href="#which-do-you-need" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Which Do You Need?</a>
          <a href="#making-a-shop-vac-better" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Upgrading a Shop Vac</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. The Verdict</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A dust extractor costs three to five times a shop vac and often moves less air. What it does instead is hold that air — pulsing its filter clean every few seconds so the machine performs identically in hour three and minute one. Whether that is worth the money depends entirely on what you are cutting.
        </p>

        <div id="the-difference" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Separates a Dust Extractor From a Shop Vac
          </h2>

          <p>
            They look similar, they both suck, and one costs three to five times what the other does. The difference is not power — many shop vacs move more air than a comparable extractor. It is that an extractor is designed to hold performance while collecting fine dust, and to capture that dust rather than redistribute it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three features define the category. <strong>Automatic filter cleaning</strong> pulses air backward through the filter every few seconds, knocking the dust cake off before it can blind the pleats. <strong>Certified filtration</strong> to a published standard means the fine fraction is actually captured rather than exhausted. And <strong>variable suction with a tool-triggered outlet</strong> means the machine matches the tool it is attached to and starts with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A shop vac can do any single one of those jobs adequately with the right accessories. What it cannot do is hold full airflow through an hour of sanding, which is the entire point of the more expensive machine.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Distinction in One Sentence</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A shop vac collects debris; a dust extractor controls dust at the source while you make it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">That is why an extractor is worth the money to someone sanding drywall all day and irrelevant to someone clearing leaves out of a garage twice a year.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="filter-cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Automatic Filter Cleaning Is the Real Feature
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the capability people underestimate before they own one and rate first afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a shop vac collecting fine dust, airflow falls continuously from the moment you start. A cartridge caked with drywall or concrete dust can lose half the machine's airflow in twenty minutes, and you adapt to the decline without noticing until the tool stops extracting properly and dust starts escaping at the shroud.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dust extractor pulses air back through the filter automatically — typically every fifteen to thirty seconds, or on demand — which drops the dust cake into the tank and restores airflow. The result is a machine that performs the same in hour three as in minute one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical consequence is not convenience but dust control. Extraction at the tool only works while airflow is high; once the filter blinds, the shroud stops capturing and the dust goes into the room instead. That is why professionals on silica-generating work use extractors rather than shop vacs, and why regulations covering that work specify filtration and airflow performance rather than simply 'a vacuum'.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="filtration-standards" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Filtration: Published Standards vs Marketing Words
          </h2>

          <p>
            The other half of the price difference is what happens to the finest particles.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Machine</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Filtration</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Suitable For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basic shop vac, standard filter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Coarse debris only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Garage and yard cleanup</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shop vac, fine dust cartridge + bag</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most sanding and household dust</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">DIY woodwork, indoor cleanup</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shop vac, HEPA cartridge + bag</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine wood dust, most silica</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Occasional masonry work</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dust extractor, certified HEPA</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Certified capture, tested as a system</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Daily silica and fine dust work</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The important word is <strong>certified</strong>. A HEPA-rated cartridge fitted to a shop vac filters well, but the machine around it is not tested as a sealed system, so some air can bypass the filter through the lid seal and the hose cuff. A dust extractor is designed, gasketed and certified as a whole.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a homeowner sanding a bedroom ceiling twice a decade, a HEPA cartridge and a bag in a good shop vac is a sensible and proportionate answer. For a contractor cutting concrete every week, it is not, and the difference is a health outcome rather than a convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be sceptical of 'HEPA-style' and 'HEPA-like' on any filter. Those phrases mean the product resembles something certified without being it, and the entire value of buying a HEPA filter is the certification.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Each One Costs, Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            The price gap is real and the running costs diverge as well.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Shop Vac</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Dust Extractor</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Purchase</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$60-$400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$400-$1,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Filters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$12-$70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$60-$150</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bags</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3-$6</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6-$12</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stiff, corrugated, often included</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anti-static, smooth bore, premium</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Noise</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">75-90 dB</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">62-75 dB — noticeably quieter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wet pickup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, on nearly all models</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Many, but check — some are dry only</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Noise is an underrated line in that table. Dust extractors are built to run continuously beside a person using a power tool, and the good ones are quiet enough to hold a conversation over. A shop vac at 88 dB running for an eight-hour day is genuinely unpleasant and a hearing risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hose difference matters too. Extractor hoses are anti-static, which stops the static build-up that makes a shop vac hose cling to everything and occasionally deliver a shock, and smooth-bore, which loses less airflow over a long run.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against all of that, a shop vac does things an extractor often will not: pick up water, swallow bulky debris, tolerate abuse, and cost very little. Many workshops end up with both, which is a rational outcome rather than an indulgence.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            There is a third practical difference that rarely appears in comparisons: how each machine behaves when the bag fills. A shop vac with a full bag simply loses suction, gradually, and you find out when the tool stops extracting. Most extractors monitor airflow and warn you — some reduce suction deliberately or display an alert — which matters when the consequence of unnoticed airflow loss is dust in your lungs rather than a slower cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Variable suction is similarly underrated. On a sander, full suction pulls the pad against the workpiece and makes the tool harder to control; dialling the extractor back restores the feel while still capturing dust. Shop vacs run at one speed, and the usual workaround is a bleed valve in the hose, which works but wastes airflow.
          </p>

        <div id="which-do-you-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which One Do You Actually Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The decision is about how often you generate fine dust, not about how serious a woodworker you consider yourself.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your Work</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Buy</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garage cleanup, leaves, car, spills</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shop vac</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bulk debris and water are its strengths</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Occasional DIY sanding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shop vac + fine cartridge + bags</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Proportionate, and far cheaper</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Regular woodworking with power tools</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either; extractor if budget allows</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Auto filter cleaning saves real frustration</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Frequent drywall or plaster work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dust extractor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shop vac filters blind within minutes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete, tile or masonry cutting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dust extractor, certified HEPA</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silica exposure is a health issue, not a nuisance</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Renovation contractor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Both</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Extractor for dust, shop vac for debris and water</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The honest middle ground for most keen DIYers is a good shop vac plus a bag plus a fine dust cartridge plus a habit of tapping the filter between sessions. That combination handles everything a home workshop generates, at a fraction of the cost, with a little more attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            The point at which an extractor stops being a luxury is when you are doing fine dust work for hours at a time rather than minutes, or when the dust contains silica. Both change the calculation from convenience to health.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="making-a-shop-vac-better" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Getting Extractor Behaviour From a Shop Vac
          </h2>

          <p>
            Several accessories close much of the gap for a modest outlay.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Cyclone Separator</h3>
          <p>
            A cyclonic pre-separator sits between the hose and the vac and drops the great majority of debris into a bucket before it ever reaches the filter. For woodworking this is transformative: the filter stays clean for far longer, airflow holds up, and emptying is a matter of tipping a bucket. It is the single best upgrade for a shop vac used on sawdust.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Collection Bags and a Fine Cartridge</h3>
          <p>
            Covered in our <a href="/shop-vac-filters-explained/">filter guide</a>, and worth repeating: a bag keeps the pleats clean and multiplies filter life, and a fine dust or HEPA cartridge captures what a standard one does not.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Tool-Triggered Outlet Adapter</h3>
          <p>
            Inline adapters exist that switch the vac on when a tool draws current. They are inexpensive and they change how often you actually bother to connect extraction, which is most of the battle.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Better Hose and Adapters</h3>
          <p>
            An anti-static, smooth-bore hose with a proper set of tool adapters improves both airflow and the experience of using it. Static cling on a stiff corrugated hose is one of the small daily irritations that makes people skip extraction entirely.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            One accessory worth adding to either machine is a longer power lead or a proper extension on 12-gauge cable. Extraction only happens when the vac reaches the work without being dragged repeatedly, and a machine on a short cord ends up unplugged and pushed into a corner. It is a trivial thing that decides whether dust control becomes a habit.
          </p>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dust extractor is a specialist tool that solves one problem extremely well: keeping airflow and filtration high while you generate fine dust for hours. If that describes your work, nothing else substitutes, and the health case for silica work makes it the only responsible choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            A shop vac is a generalist that handles debris, water, cars, gutters, workshops and spills for a fraction of the money, and with a cyclone, a bag and a fine cartridge it covers most home fine-dust work perfectly adequately.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the overwhelming majority of homeowners, the right answer is a good shop vac plus the accessories, and the money saved put toward tools. For anyone working in dust professionally, the extractor is not optional equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if you are choosing a shop vac to do double duty, prioritise the things that make it a better extractor: a large-area filter, a bag option, a 2.5-inch port with adapters, a tool-triggered outlet if available, and the lowest noise figure you can find. Our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac comparison</a> ranks current models on exactly those practical points.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Extractor for Garage Cleanup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Extractors are specialists in fine dust, and they are expensive, sometimes dry-only, and no better than a shop vac at swallowing leaves, offcuts and spilled gravel. If your work is bulk debris and the occasional spill, a shop vac is both cheaper and better suited.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Shop Vac for Daily Silica Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete, tile and mortar dust carry crystalline silica, and a shop vac's filter blinds quickly enough that the tool shroud stops capturing part way through the job. That is a health exposure, not an inconvenience, and it is the clearest case for a certified extractor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting 'HEPA-Style' Labelling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The phrase exists precisely because the product is not certified. The entire value of a HEPA filter is the published standard behind it, so buy cartridges with a stated rating from the machine's manufacturer or a brand that publishes test data.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Cyclone on a Woodworking Shop Vac</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without a pre-separator, every chip and every gram of sawdust reaches the filter, which then blinds within a session. A cyclone drops the great majority into a bucket first and is the closest a shop vac gets to extractor-like sustained airflow.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Either Machine With a Narrow Hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tool ports are restrictive enough already. Adding a 1.25-inch hose on a long run throws away much of the airflow the machine was bought for. Use the widest hose the tool port allows and keep runs as short as practical.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a shop vac and a dust extractor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A shop vac collects debris; a dust extractor controls fine dust at the source. Extractors add automatic filter cleaning that pulses the dust cake off every few seconds, certified filtration tested as a sealed system, variable suction and a tool-triggered outlet. A shop vac's filter blinds steadily on fine dust and loses airflow as it does.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dust extractor worth the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you generate fine dust for hours at a time, or work with silica-bearing materials like concrete, tile or mortar, yes — it is a health tool rather than a convenience. For occasional DIY sanding, a shop vac with a fine dust cartridge, a collection bag and a cyclone separator is a proportionate answer at a fraction of the cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a shop vac for dust collection with power tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with three caveats: use a collection bag and a fine dust cartridge, expect to clean the filter between sessions because airflow falls as it loads, and add a tool-triggered outlet adapter so extraction actually gets used. A cyclone separator ahead of the vac makes the biggest single improvement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a cyclone separator and do I need one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It sits between the hose and the vacuum and spins heavier debris out into a bucket before it reaches the filter. For woodworking it is the best upgrade available for a shop vac — the filter stays clean far longer, airflow holds up through a session, and emptying means tipping a bucket rather than handling a dusty cartridge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do dust extractors pick up water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many do, but not all — some are dry-only, and that is a genuine difference from shop vacs, where wet pickup is close to universal. If you want one machine for both fine dust extraction and water, check the specification carefully before buying rather than assuming.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are shop vac HEPA filters as good as a dust extractor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The filter media can be, but the machine around it is not certified as a sealed system, so some air can bypass through the lid seal or the hose cuff. For occasional home use that is a reasonable trade. For regular silica work, a certified extractor tested as a complete system is the appropriate tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are dust extractors so much quieter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are designed to run continuously next to someone using a power tool, so the motor housing and airflow path are engineered for noise as well as performance. Typical extractors run 62 to 75 dB against 75 to 90 dB for shop vacs — the difference between a machine you can talk over and one that needs hearing protection.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

